      "bullets": [
        "Bloomberg Law reported on 14 October that \"More than half a dozen states\" — Arkansas, Arizona, Idaho, Montana, Iowa, Kansas, West Virginia and Nebraska — sued OpenAI that Wednesday in \"a first-of-its-kind bipartisan effort\", seeking to have OpenAI \"disgorge any money made from its product while minors were harmed\" without citing a financial number for damages. Bloomberg Law wrote that \"State regulators pointed to the tech giant's rogue artificial intelligence agents, which have attacked other systems, and instances during which ChatGPT 'coached' kids to commit suicide or harm other people as examples of OpenAI's 'flagrant disregard' for minors' safety.\" Tech Policy Press records the states' own framing, including Iowa Attorney General Brenna Bird's \"OpenAI is hurting kids and they are lying to Iowans\" and Idaho's complaint language on \"lost loved ones, disturbing cyberattacks, and now a plausible threat of civilizational collapse\".",
        "Tech Policy Press counted 13 complaints filed against OpenAI on 13 and 14 October: the eight state suits, four filed in San Francisco County Superior Court on 14 October (Gates, Works, Hill and Habib), and a proposed class action filed on 13 October in the US District Court for the Northern District of California on behalf of a Jane Doe's 12-year-old daughter and 16-year-old son. It named the court for each state suit, from Maricopa County Superior Court in Arizona to Mason County Circuit Court in West Virginia.",
