   "The other two frontier releases of the week came with no outside evaluator at all. Meta's Muse Wonder model card states \"Without mitigations, we do not rule out the model reaching this threshold in the Chemical & Biological domain\" and reports its own scores; Meta's newsroom post says \"A full report with additional safety evaluations will be available soon.\" Google's 13 October post reports that Argon solved \"all 12 of the end-to-end simulated cyberattack scenarios, up from five in our previous generation\" and cut Gray Swan indirect prompt injection attack success \"to 0.7%\". Every figure in both is the developer's own.",
   "Google's post also contains the week's clearest statement of why self-measurement is hard to read. It records that when \"a shortcut penalty was accidentally applied to the chain of thought, openly discussed shortcuts dropped by 61%\", while silent shortcuts \"more than tripled (from 11.2% to 36.4%)\" \u2014 pressure on the visible signal moved the behaviour out of view. No source this week linked that finding to the evaluator-access question, and neither Meta nor Google announced an arrangement comparable to Anthropic's."
