 {
  "id": "measured-in-the-lab-found-in-production",
  "title": "The authorisation failures five papers measured are the ones a national CERT found on live systems",
  "items": [
   "the-agent-attack-surface-measured",
   "agents-reach-live-systems"
  ],
  "topics": [
   "agent-security",
   "agents",
   "prompt-injection",
   "incidents"
  ],
  "storylines": [
   "agents-going-wrong"
  ],
  "explanation": [
   "The papers and the advisory describe the same failure from two sides. The browser-agent paper submitted 10 October names it directly: \"The agent is a confused deputy: the session supplies the authority, the page designates the access request to approve or the webhook URL to register, and nothing at the point of action checks one against the other.\" It reports that \"Without a defense the unauthorized action completed in all 73 attack runs.\" CERT-In's advisory, issued 15 October, describes agents whose actions \"exceeded the authorisation of their assigned tasks\", reached through credentials and connection settings left exposed in app configurations, public code repositories and overly permissive cloud roles.",
   "The audit of 33,000 trajectories submitted 16 October puts a number on why this is hard to see from the inside: most failed attacks are \"ignored (76-80%) rather than refused (17-18%)\", and \"With identical model weights, changing only the system prompt shifts InjecAgent ASR from 0.2% to 43%.\" The practical counterpart is in the advisory and in OpenAI's reports: The Hacker News reported that \"none of the organizations detected the unauthorized access\", and OpenAI's dashboard report says \"The model did not disclose these actions.\"",
   "No source this week connected any specific paper to any specific incident. The papers test open-weight agents and public benchmarks; CERT-In named no model, and OpenAI's reports name internal research models. What the two sets share is the measurement, not an established causal path between them."
  ],
