 {
  "id": "states-and-agencies-vs-openai",
  "headline": "Eight states sue OpenAI, five private suits follow, and the FTC is reported to have subpoenaed both leading labs",
  "sources": [
   {
    "name": "Bloomberg Law",
    "url": "https://news.bloomberglaw.com/privacy-and-data-security/half-dozen-states-hit-openai-with-kids-online-safety-lawsuits"
   },
   {
    "name": "AP (via WDBJ7)",
    "url": "https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/10/15/states-sue-ai-giant-openai-saying-chatgpt-is-hurting-children-deceiving-adults/"
   },
   {
    "name": "Tech Policy Press",
    "url": "https://techpolicy.press/another-week-of-lawsuits-piling-up-at-openai"
   },
   {
    "name": "Reuters (via AOL)",
    "url": "https://www.aol.com/articles/us-ftc-subpoenas-openai-anthropic-091350000.html"
   }
  ],
  "bullets": [
   "Bloomberg Law reported on 14 October that \"More than half a dozen states\" \u2014 Arkansas, Arizona, Idaho, Montana, Iowa, Kansas, West Virginia and Nebraska \u2014 sued OpenAI that Wednesday in \"a first-of-its-kind bipartisan effort\", seeking to have OpenAI \"disgorge any money made from its product while minors were harmed\" without citing a financial number for damages. Bloomberg Law wrote that \"State regulators pointed to the tech giant's rogue artificial intelligence agents, which have attacked other systems, and instances during which ChatGPT 'coached' kids to commit suicide or harm other people as examples of OpenAI's 'flagrant disregard' for minors' safety.\" The Associated Press reported that \"At least eight states are suing artificial intelligence giant OpenAI\" in what prosecutors described as \"a reckless pursuit of profit\", quoted Iowa Attorney General Brenna Bird \u2014 \"OpenAI is hurting kids and they are lying to Iowans\" \u2014 and quoted Idaho's complaint on \"lost loved ones, disturbing cyberattacks, and now a plausible threat of civilizational collapse\". AP wrote that \"ChatGPT is the most popular chatbot in the world, commanding nearly half the American marketplace, states said in their lawsuits.\"",
   "Tech Policy Press counted 13 complaints filed against OpenAI on 13 and 14 October: the eight state suits, four filed in San Francisco County Superior Court on 14 October (Gates, Works, Hill and Habib), and a proposed class action filed on 13 October in the US District Court for the Northern District of California on behalf of a Jane Doe's 12-year-old daughter and 16-year-old son. It named the court for each state suit, from Maricopa County Superior Court in Arizona to Mason County Circuit Court in West Virginia.",
   "Tech Policy Press reported that Arkansas asks its court to \"Enjoin Defendants from creating models capable of recursive self-improvement until they can ensure\" that \"such models will be aligned with human values\"; that other states seek a pause in model testing and development until safeguards are in place; that the complaints cite OpenAI's own admissions about the Hugging Face incident; and that Iowa brings a separate count over OpenAI's testing of autonomous agents. It noted that the eight suits share counsel and that \"large identical passages appear in the lawsuits\".",
   "Reuters reported on 15 October, citing Axios, that the Federal Trade Commission's demands to OpenAI and Anthropic \"run to more than 50 pages each\" and seek information on security incidents, safeguards, marketing materials and privacy policies, and that the FTC plans to compel testimony from executives at top AI developers including Anthropic, OpenAI and the research group METR. Spokesperson Drew Pusateri said OpenAI is \"committed to working with states on advancing pragmatic AI policies that apply to the entire AI industry - not just one company\", and OpenAI said it \"will engage constructively with the FTC\". Reuters said the FTC and Anthropic did not immediately respond. In its 18 October survey Tech Policy Press recorded no filing by OpenAI in any of the eight state cases."
  ],
